  "text": "Since antiquity, scholars have examined how language should be clear, precise, fitting, and lively, and they recognized metaphor as a key stylistic device. Over the centuries, thinkers in various cultures refined ideas about style, warning that poor word choices can block comprehension and that excessive reliance on decorative language can weaken a message. They argued that a speaker's style reflects the way thoughts arise in the mind and that rhetorical figures must match the subject rather than artificially elevate it. By the 19th century, systematic guidelines for using such devices were being taught, emphasizing appropriate selection and cautioning against overuse. In more recent times, attention to these figures has faded; a review of several public‑speaking textbooks showed that many omit discussion of them, while those that include them do so only briefly or as part of broader language‑choice advice."
